  │ [38;5;153mstore/local.js[39m      │ localStorage implementation                                 │ store interface │
  ├─────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mui/*.js[39m             │ Task list, inbox, toasts, badge                             │ model, store    │
  ├─────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mapp.js[39m              │ Wiring, tick loop, event plumbing                           │ everything      │
  └─────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────┘

  The line that matters: [1m[38;5;153mmodel/[39m and [38;5;153mengine/[39m import no DOM and no storage.[0m [38;5;153mevaluate()[39m is a pure function of (tasks,
  already-seen keys, current time) → new events. That makes every hard behavior — catch-up after a long absence, DST
  boundaries, a due date edited after firing — a unit test with a fake clock rather than a thing you verify by waiting.

  [38;5;153mengine/clock.js[39m exists solely so tests can control time. It's three lines, and without it the test suite would have to
  sleep.

  [1mStorage seam:[0m v1 uses localStorage behind an async interface. It's synchronous underneath, but the interface is async
  so swapping to IndexedDB later is a one-module change that ripples nowhere. localStorage's ~5MB cap is a real
  constraint for an append-only log, so the design includes a retention policy (Section 2) rather than pretending the
  log is free.

  Alongside the architecture, the tooling question — this is cheapest to answer now, before any code exists, and
  whatever you pick lands in the spec's constraints so every later task inherits it:
